{"pdfpcFormat":1,"disableMarkdown":false,"noteFontSize":20,"pages":[{"idx":0,"label":"1","overlay":0,"note":"Welcome to this presentation\n\nToday, I'm talking about the possibility of performing cryptographic operations through OP-TEE with an on-die secure enclave."},{"idx":1,"label":"2","overlay":0,"note":"I'm an Embedded Linux engineer at Bootlin for eighteen months now.\n\nWe help clients to introduce Linux and open-source software into their embedded devices.\n\nBefore that, I worked for several years for an IoT operator.\n\nAnd I live close to Toulouse."},{"idx":2,"label":"3","overlay":0,"note":"This presentation is divided into two main parts:\n\nFirst, I introduce OP-TEE, PKCS#11 and the i.MX8 SoC family.\n\nIn the second part, I explain how to build a Yocto distribution including the required customizations and software components to perform PKCS#11 operations on i.MX8 through OP-TEE.\n\nIn my example, I assume that the BSP provides everything needed to use OP-TEE."},{"idx":4,"label":"5","overlay":0,"note":"Here, the goal is to be able to perform OpenSSL operations without using a secure element, an HSM, a TPM, and so on, while isolating secrets from the rest of the system and controlling access to them.\n\nTo do that, the idea is to make them accessible only in secure mode: the devices used to store keys and perform crypto operations can only be accessed from OP-TEE."},{"idx":5,"label":"6","overlay":0,"note":"PKCS#eleven is a cryptography standard that defines a platform-independent API, with callbacks to create, manage and store keys and certificates.\n\nIt allows using the same software stack on several kinds of secure modules, such as Hardware Security Modules, Trusted Platform Modules, and so on.\n\nThis API can be used with some open-source software libraries and tools such as OpenSSL, OpenSSH...\n"},{"idx":6,"label":"7","overlay":0,"note":"OP-TEE is a Trusted Execution Environment: a\ncompanion OS that is isolated from Linux and can be used to perform some operations or to access critical devices.\n\nIts purpose is to add a protection layer against Linux security vulnerabilities, especially 0-days.\n\nOP-TEE and Linux communicate through the Secure Monitor, which makes the link between the Normal world and the Secure world.\nHere, the Secure Monitor is provided by the Trusted Firmware (BL thirty one).\n\nOP-TEE can execute applications, named Trusted Applications, which can be either included in the OP-TEE OS binary or provided from userspace.\n\nAnd Trusted Applications can be signed."},{"idx":7,"label":"8","overlay":0,"note":"The i.MX eight SoC family provides a few interesting features that can be used through OP-TEE, in our case to isolate keys and to perform crypto operations.\n\nHardware isolation is based on ARM TrustZone, and secure boot on the NXP High Assurance Boot, which allows signing and also encrypting bootloader stages, including OP-TEE.\n\nIt provides a crypto accelerator and a non-volatile storage, which are protected by hardware anti-tampering features, and which can also be managed by OP-TEE instead of Linux."},{"idx":8,"label":"9","overlay":0,"note":"To use OP-TEE on i.MX eight SoC familly, it is necessary to use an SPL stage because the ROM code isn't able to load a boot container that contains additional binaries, such as OP-TEE.\nSo, maybe you will need to modify your BSP.\n\nThe boot sequence or bootloader image formats may be different, depending on the platform.\n\nSome platforms use FIP, which is a generic way proposed by Trusted Firmware, but it isn't the case on i.MX eight.\n\nSo, the SPL will start TF-A,  then TF-A will start OP-TEE and finally U-Boot.\n\nHere, boot stages can also be signed and encrypted with NXP Code Signing Tools."},{"idx":10,"label":"11","overlay":0,"note":"Here, I assume that the BSP provides everything needed to use optee.\n\nTo be able to perform PKCS eleven operations with OpenSSL through OP-TEE, it is necessary to install a set of libraries and utilities.\n\nOpenSC provides a library, an engine, that allows the OpenSSL library to access PKCS#11 modules.\n\nlibckteec provides the TEE Client API library that allows serializing and deserializing arguments sent to Trusted Applications.\n\nOP-TEE client provides the normal world userspace client APIs for OP-TEE.\n\nWe also need to install Trusted Applications, especially the one that allows to perform PKCS eleven operations from OP-TEE.\n\nFinally, because OpenSSL doesn't provide an interface to initialize or import a PKCS eleven token, it is also necessary to install the pkcs eleven tools.\n\n"},{"idx":11,"label":"12","overlay":0,"note":"Some Yocto BSP layers provide an optee machine feature to enable or disable OP-TEE support on the selected target.\n\nSo, in this case, it is only necessary to enable the optee DISTRO_FEATURES, and the BSP build parameters will be updated.\nThen the SPL and OP-TEE will be built, and TF-A will be rebuilt with additional build parameters.\n\nFinally, to be able to perform pkcs eleven operations, the packages identified above need to be installed in the rootfs image.\n\nHere, OP-TEE functional tests are also installed to be able to perform some verifications before creating a key.\n"},{"idx":12,"label":"13","overlay":0,"note":"\nIt is possible that the mainline BSP of your i.MX8 target doesn't provide OP-TEE support, but it is possible to use the vendor BSP by setting the variable IMX_DEFAUT_BSP.\n\nThe OP-TEE PKCS eleven support may not be enabled by default, so it is possible to override the OP-TEE compilation flags with an additional parameter to enable it.\n\nIt is also possible to increase the OP-TEE log level to debug, and to append the key used to sign Trusted Applications, so that the integrity of a Trusted Application is checked before it is executed.\n\nAnd if you use a custom SoM or board, you may need to modify some default platform configuration, such as the UART.\n"},{"idx":13,"label":"14","overlay":0,"note":"Here are a few examples of commands that can be used to check that OP-TEE is correctly probed and usable from the Linux userspace.\n\nWe can see that the OP-TEE driver is correctly probed and the interfaces have been mounted.\n\nHere, we also run the provided OP-TEE functional tests."},{"idx":14,"label":"15","overlay":0,"note":"Here, with this command, we check that OP-TEE is reachable through the cryptoki API and is therefore usable to perform PKCS eleven operations."},{"idx":15,"label":"16","overlay":0,"note":"Here is an example of a few commands to create a token that will be stored in the non-volatile storage provided by the i.MX eight.\n"},{"idx":16,"label":"17","overlay":0,"note":"Here, we check that the token has been created correctly, with the correct flags."},{"idx":17,"label":"18","overlay":0,"note":"Finally, here we create a key with OpenSSL over PKCS eleven and OP-TEE."},{"idx":18,"label":"19","overlay":0,"note":"Do you have questions?"}]}